💡 BEST PRACTICES FOR LIKE OPERATOR
═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

1. PERFORMANCE OPTIMIZATION
   ───────────────────────────
   ✓ GOOD:   SELECT * FROM Customers WHERE City LIKE 'berlin%';
   ✗ AVOID:  SELECT * FROM Customers WHERE City LIKE '%berlin%';
   
   WHY: Leading wildcard (%) prevents index usage. Trailing % allows index usage.
   IMPACT: Can dramatically improve query speed on large tables


2. UNDERSTAND WILDCARD POSITIONS
   ──────────────────────────────
   Pattern              Effect
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   'text%'              Starts with (good for index)
   '%text'              Ends with (no index support)
   '%text%'             Contains (no index support)
   '_text%'             2nd position specific
   'text_%'             At least N characters


3. COMBINE WITH INDEXES
   ───────────────────────
   • LIKE with leading characters can use indexes: LIKE 'A%' ✓
   • LIKE with leading wildcard cannot: LIKE '%A' ✗
   • Use FULLTEXT search for very large text searches


4. USE NOT LIKE CAREFULLY
   ────────────────────────
   NOT LIKE typically doesn't use indexes efficiently
   Consider using alternative conditions for large datasets:
   
   ✗ Slower:  SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Email NOT LIKE '%@gmail.com'
   ✓ Better:  SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Email NOT LIKE '%@gmail.com' 
              AND Email IS NOT NULL


5. ESCAPE SPECIAL CHARACTERS
   ─────────────────────────
   If searching for literal % or _ characters:
   
   SELECT * FROM Products WHERE Name LIKE '%50\%%';     -- Finds "50%"
   SELECT * FROM Products WHERE Name LIKE '%\_' ESCAPE '\';  -- Finds "_"


6. COMBINE MULTIPLE CONDITIONS
   ────────────────────────────
   Use AND/OR strategically to narrow results:
   
   ✓ Good order of conditions:
   SELECT * FROM Customers 
   WHERE Country = 'USA'              -- Most restrictive first
   AND City LIKE 'new%'               -- Less restrictive
   AND CustomerName LIKE '%a%';       -- Least restrictive


7. REMEMBER NULL VALUES
   ────────────────────
   NULL does NOT match any LIKE pattern:
   
   SELECT * FROM Customers WHERE City LIKE 'berlin%';
   
   Result will NOT include rows where City IS NULL
   To include nulls:
   SELECT * FROM Customers WHERE City LIKE 'berlin%' OR City IS NULL;


8. USE COLLATIONS FOR SPECIFIC BEHAVIOR
   ─────────────────────────────────────
   Control case sensitivity and accent matching:
   
   SELECT * FROM Customers WHERE CustomerName LIKE 'José%' 
   COLLATE utf8mb4_unicode_ci;


